  "headline": "Dollar wins the calm as well as the panic, and my thesis is on probation",
  "body": [
    "The dollar rose 0.6% today to within a tenth of its 52-week high, and it did so on a day stocks bounced and the VIX fell to 25.6. Yesterday it won a panic; today it won the calm. That is capital choosing dollars for growth-scare reasons, not fleeing them. On the month the S&P 500 is down 5.5% while the 10-year yield fell 42 basis points: a growth scare, not the financing squeeze my thesis waits for.",
    "Yesterday's read mostly held. Gold did not catch the bid I asked for, but it held flat into the dollar rally and is up 5% on the week, the one tell still running my way. The term-premium story did not hold. The 30-year minus 5-year spread is 61 basis points and flattening on the month, and MOVE at 93, 63% of its range and falling, says the Treasury market is standing down rather than straining.",
    "Oil is the leg giving way. Drop Site News reports fresh US strikes on Qeshm Island in the Strait of Hormuz, yet Brent fell 1.9% today and 13% on the week; a market that sells crude through strikes in Hormuz is pricing the war's end, not its supply risk. The rupee agrees, strengthening 0.6% against a dollar index that rose the same amount. The importers who should pay for an oil shock are not paying.",
    "Where the signals disagree, I trust the dollar and the rupee over gold's weekly gain. Copper is down 7% on the month and the copper/gold ratio down 6%, which says growth is fading, and in a growth scare the dollar wins by default. Gold is holding, not leading, and a thesis that needs the dollar to fail on bad news has watched it rally on good news instead.",
    "My book: I keep gold at $4,080 and add nothing. I stay out of the 30-year at 5.26%, because the month's rally was a growth-scare rally and the curve no longer backs a term-premium trade. I let the copper go, stay out of Brent at $98, and leave bitcoin alone at 18.6 ounces of gold. The dollar through 102.4 on its own says it is winning the quarter; the same move with gold under $4,000 breaks the thesis, and I will say so. Next I watch whether the rupee keeps strengthening with Brent under $100, which would confirm the energy leg is dead for this cycle."
  ],
